WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.060 --> 00:00:04.300
On the 11th of December 1936, a man spoke
on the BBC.

00:00:04.300 --> 00:00:08.520
Millions of listeners held their breath as
he announced his resolve to go forward with

00:00:08.520 --> 00:00:13.980
a decision which was, and remains, unique
in British history: his abdication to the

00:00:13.980 --> 00:00:14.980
throne.

00:00:14.980 --> 00:00:16.410
That man was Edward VIII.

00:00:16.410 --> 00:00:20.570
The reason behind his abdication was his love
for a woman that the British Court and the

00:00:20.570 --> 00:00:25.990
establishment would have never accepted as
a Queen: the American, twice divorced, Wallis

00:00:25.990 --> 00:00:26.990
Simpson.

00:00:26.990 --> 00:00:29.300
In today’s Biographics we are going to present
their story.

00:00:29.300 --> 00:00:33.100
One of frustration, unhappy marriages and
fraught relationships.

00:00:33.100 --> 00:00:37.770
The story of how a man and a woman renounced
to the British Crown, apparently for love,

00:00:37.770 --> 00:00:42.260
and of how they were tempted to reclaim the
throne by the machinations of their supposed

00:00:42.260 --> 00:00:51.800
enemy, the Third Reich.

00:00:51.800 --> 00:00:58.780
Edward
Edward was born on the 23rd of June 1894 in

00:00:58.780 --> 00:00:59.790
Richmond, Surrey.

00:00:59.790 --> 00:01:03.850
He was the eldest child of the future King
George V, the grandchild to King Edward VII,

00:01:03.850 --> 00:01:09.280
and a great-grandchild to Queen Victoria,
who was still alive at the time of his birth.

00:01:09.280 --> 00:01:13.899
He was always known in his family as David,
the last of his many middle names.

00:01:13.899 --> 00:01:16.170
From this youth, Edward was set on a naval
career.

00:01:16.170 --> 00:01:20.350
However, the quick ascension to the throne
of his father George V in 1910 meant that

00:01:20.350 --> 00:01:24.189
Edward was immediately appointed Prince of
Wales on his 16th birthday.

00:01:24.189 --> 00:01:27.259
Edward had to quit his naval studies and enrolled
at Oxford.

00:01:27.259 --> 00:01:32.619
It’s generally agreed that Edward was intellectually
underwhelming, or maybe just not that interested

00:01:32.619 --> 00:01:33.619
in studying.

00:01:33.619 --> 00:01:37.770
Besides a good performance with his polo team
at Oxford, he had little academic achievement

00:01:37.770 --> 00:01:41.420
and left university after eight terms with
no degree.

00:01:41.420 --> 00:01:44.409
At the outbreak of WWI Edward joined the Grenadier
Guards.

00:01:44.409 --> 00:01:48.789
The young Prince was keen to see action on
the Western front, like his rival, counterpart

00:01:48.789 --> 00:01:51.490
and relative, the German Crown Prince Wilhelm.

00:01:51.490 --> 00:01:56.189
However, the British Government forbade this,
for the risk of losing the heir to the throne.

00:01:56.189 --> 00:02:01.409
After the war, in January 1919, Edward’s
youngest brother, the Prince John, died aged

00:02:01.409 --> 00:02:02.439
13.

00:02:02.439 --> 00:02:06.350
After a long struggle with epilepsy, the young
prince had succumbed to a seizure.

00:02:06.350 --> 00:02:08.710
On this occasion Edward referred to John’s
death as

00:02:08.710 --> 00:02:11.930
“little more than a regrettable nuisance.”

00:02:11.930 --> 00:02:15.909
His contacts with John were so limited due
to his illness, that he remembered him as

00:02:15.909 --> 00:02:19.739
“more of an animal than anything else”
Edward had to write a letter to his mother,

00:02:19.739 --> 00:02:24.120
apologising for being such a
“cold-hearted and unsympathetic swine”

00:02:24.120 --> 00:02:28.610
Throughout the 1920s, Edward undertook extensive
foreign tours particularly in the British

00:02:28.610 --> 00:02:30.060
Empire, representing his father.

00:02:30.060 --> 00:02:35.180
Edward also took to visiting areas of high
unemployment and deprivation in Britain, during

00:02:35.180 --> 00:02:37.950
the economic depression of the early 1930s.

00:02:37.950 --> 00:02:42.189
These occasions made his figure familiar and
popular with the press and the public.

00:02:42.189 --> 00:02:46.500
His image at this stage was that of a dashing
dandy: good-looking, well dressed, skilled

00:02:46.500 --> 00:02:48.629
at playing polo and flying airplanes.

00:02:48.629 --> 00:02:52.650
He had earned a pilot’s licence in 1918
and was the first British monarch to ever

00:02:52.650 --> 00:02:53.650
fly an aircraft.

00:02:53.650 --> 00:02:57.000
In the 1920s Edward also dedicated time to
another passion.

00:02:57.000 --> 00:03:01.650
He had a string of affairs with several married
women, which cemented his status as the World’s

00:03:01.650 --> 00:03:02.760
most desirable bachelor.

00:03:02.760 --> 00:03:07.310
At the same time, his affairs seemed to keep
him from fulfilling one of his duties as Prince

00:03:07.310 --> 00:03:10.090
of Wales: finding a suitable match.

00:03:10.090 --> 00:03:15.700
In late 1930, Edward had started a relationship
with Thelma, Viscountess Furness, also married

00:03:15.700 --> 00:03:16.700
at the time.

00:03:16.700 --> 00:03:21.410
It was Thelma who, probably in January 1931
introduced her to a beautiful, intelligent

00:03:21.410 --> 00:03:26.769
and witty American friend, Wallis Simpson.

00:03:26.769 --> 00:03:32.510
Wallis[TA1]
Wallis Simpson was born Bessie-Wallis Warfield

00:03:32.510 --> 00:03:36.379
on the 19th of June, 1896, in Blue Ridge Summit,
Pennsylvania.

00:03:36.379 --> 00:03:41.820
This was a SPA town where her father Teackle
Wallis was seeking treatment for tuberculosis,

00:03:41.820 --> 00:03:45.269
which unfortunately claimed his life when
Bessie was only a few months old.

00:03:45.269 --> 00:03:49.219
Alice, Bessie’s mother, was an impoverished
widow, but fortunately she was taken in by

00:03:49.219 --> 00:03:53.181
Solomon, her late husband’s rich brother,
living in Baltimore.

00:03:53.181 --> 00:03:57.610
Solomon also paid for Bessie-Wallis’ education
in an exclusive all-girl private school.

00:03:57.610 --> 00:04:02.030
As a teenager in Baltimore, Wallis dropped
the ‘Bessie’ and was subject to many girlhood

00:04:02.030 --> 00:04:03.030
passions.

00:04:03.030 --> 00:04:06.709
She fell madly in love with two teachers and
several girlfriends and sent them letters

00:04:06.709 --> 00:04:09.620
describing them as “beautiful little partridges”.

00:04:09.620 --> 00:04:13.590
There is no proof that she may have had homosexual
relationships, though.

00:04:13.590 --> 00:04:18.389
After graduating, Wallis dated many handsome
men, despite not being well-off nor especially

00:04:18.389 --> 00:04:20.489
good looking by the standards of that time.

00:04:20.489 --> 00:04:23.810
In 1916, Wallis married an airman called Win
Spencer.

00:04:23.810 --> 00:04:26.140
The marriage was not a happy one.

00:04:26.140 --> 00:04:29.780
When Win tried to kiss her on their wedding
night, Wallis recoiled.

00:04:29.780 --> 00:04:32.440
Later she asserted that the marriage was never
consummated.

00:04:32.440 --> 00:04:35.300
The marriage was doomed to failure.

00:04:35.300 --> 00:04:39.250
Win was transferred to China, where their
relationship eventually broke down, because

00:04:39.250 --> 00:04:41.449
of Win’s alcoholism and sadism.

00:04:41.449 --> 00:04:45.020
Wallis left the household and went to live
with her friend Katherine and her husband,

00:04:45.020 --> 00:04:46.020
Herman Rogers.

00:04:46.020 --> 00:04:51.100
Herman was not just a friend, Wallis considered
him to be the true love of her life.

00:04:51.100 --> 00:04:55.090
Although according to her biographer Andrew
Morton, also this relationship never evolved

00:04:55.090 --> 00:04:56.180
into a sexual one.

00:04:56.180 --> 00:05:01.080
Later, she met a South American man, Felipe
Espil, who also undoubtedly became the true

00:05:01.080 --> 00:05:05.330
love of Wallis’s life, though she definitely
never had sex with him either.

00:05:05.330 --> 00:05:09.389
Wallis then moved to England where she married
Anglo-American businessman Ernest Simpson.

00:05:09.389 --> 00:05:13.949
Ernest may not have shared Win’ alcoholism
nor sadism, but also this second marriage

00:05:13.949 --> 00:05:15.919
was a platonic relationship.

00:05:15.919 --> 00:05:19.419
Again, this is Wallis’ own version as reported
by Morton.

00:05:19.419 --> 00:05:23.580
In London, Wallis became friends with Thelma
Viscountess Furness, who introduced her to

00:05:23.580 --> 00:05:24.580
Edward.

00:05:24.580 --> 00:05:28.729
From then on Wallis became a regular presence
of the royal social scene and she started

00:05:28.729 --> 00:05:33.199
to receive letters signed simply ‘PW’
– Prince of Wales.

00:05:33.199 --> 00:05:40.650
Wallis and Edward[TA2]
When the affair between Thelma and the Prince

00:05:40.650 --> 00:05:43.770
of Wales came to an end, Wallis took her friend’s
place.

00:05:43.770 --> 00:05:47.250
Very early in the relationship, Wallis became
indispensable to his household.

00:05:47.250 --> 00:05:51.290
She organised his dinner parties and went
on exotic foreign holidays with him.

00:05:51.290 --> 00:05:56.030
Royal courtiers were not openly hostile at
this stage, yet they were wary of Wallis’

00:05:56.030 --> 00:05:57.030
hold on the Prince.

00:05:57.030 --> 00:06:01.000
Again, evidence suggests that their relationship
was not of a physical nature, and there are

00:06:01.000 --> 00:06:02.811
questions about the true nature of their attraction.

00:06:02.811 --> 00:06:03.811
Wallis may have been attracted only by the
proximity to power, while Edward may have

00:06:03.811 --> 00:06:04.811
seen in Wallis, a twice divorcee, a way out
from his future duties as a King.

00:06:04.811 --> 00:06:09.550
But Edward soon had to face reality: on the
29th of January 1936 George VI died after

00:06:09.550 --> 00:06:10.850
a prolonged illness.

00:06:10.850 --> 00:06:13.410
It was time for his Accession to the throne.

00:06:13.410 --> 00:06:17.350
The new King did not have any intention to
interrupt his love story with Wallis and the

00:06:17.350 --> 00:06:22.889
two continued to be seen together in official
occasions, such as the Proclamation of his

00:06:22.889 --> 00:06:23.889
own Accession.

00:06:23.889 --> 00:06:27.480
At this stage their affair was not widely
known to the public, as British establishment

00:06:27.480 --> 00:06:29.699
did their best to hush up the whole story.

00:06:29.699 --> 00:06:33.520
But members of the high society had started
spreading stories which made their way to

00:06:33.520 --> 00:06:35.370
the US and other countries.

00:06:35.370 --> 00:06:39.979
Readers abroad started to collect news clippings
from gossip columns and post them to relatives

00:06:39.979 --> 00:06:41.039
in the UK.

00:06:41.039 --> 00:06:44.180
In other cases, foreign magazines carrying
the news were sold in London.

00:06:44.180 --> 00:06:50.090
An article from the Milwaukee Journal, dated
30th of October 1936 tells how American newspapers

00:06:50.090 --> 00:06:53.600
were mysteriously removed from newsstands
in central London.

00:06:53.600 --> 00:06:57.009
Sometimes, just a selection of pages was ripped
out.

00:06:57.009 --> 00:07:00.690
After a while, the whole world seemed to be
in on the story – except for the British

00:07:00.690 --> 00:07:04.650
press who feigned ignorance or just chose
to ignore the affair.

00:07:04.650 --> 00:07:06.180
When Wallis finally divorced her husband,
a modern-day journalist would have made a

00:07:06.180 --> 00:07:08.919
field day out of it: this meant that Wallis
and Edward were one step closer to marrying.

00:07:08.919 --> 00:07:14.400
And yet it was covered by a single article,
at the bottom of page 10 in The Guardian.

00:07:14.400 --> 00:07:18.550
The story of the affair between the King and
a twice-divorcée American finally exploded

00:07:18.550 --> 00:07:24.200
in December 1936 when Bishop Blunt of Bradford
denounced the couple from his pulpit.

00:07:24.200 --> 00:07:28.129
With the cat out of the bag, and with Wallis’
divorce out of the way, a word started to

00:07:28.129 --> 00:07:33.639
make the rounds amongst the British Cabinet
of Ministers: abdication.

00:07:33.639 --> 00:07:37.960
As King of Great Britain and Northern Ireland,
Edward was also head of the Church of England.

00:07:37.960 --> 00:07:42.710
At that time the Church did not allow for
divorcees to re-marry, unless their former

00:07:42.710 --> 00:07:44.220
spouse was already dead.

00:07:44.220 --> 00:07:48.970
So, could the head of the Anglican faith violate
one of the tenets of his own Church?

00:07:48.970 --> 00:07:52.939
For this reason, the Cabinet feared that the
British people would never accept Wallis as

00:07:52.939 --> 00:07:53.939
Queen.

00:07:53.939 --> 00:07:55.080
Edward had to choose: either Wallis, or the
Crown.

00:07:55.080 --> 00:07:59.379
But actually, at the beginning he tried to
negotiate with the Cabinet.

00:07:59.379 --> 00:08:04.349
He had already proposed the idea of a morganatic
marriage to Prime Minister Baldwin, in November

00:08:04.349 --> 00:08:05.530
1936.

00:08:05.530 --> 00:08:09.610
A morganatic marriage would leave Edward on
the throne, assign to Wallis a lesser title

00:08:09.610 --> 00:08:13.800
instead of queen, and would have revoked rights
of succession to any offspring.

00:08:13.800 --> 00:08:19.940
When Baldwin rejected the proposal, Edward
had only one option left.

00:08:19.940 --> 00:08:25.539
Abdication[TA3]
On the 10th of December 1936, King Edward

00:08:25.539 --> 00:08:29.830
VIII submitted his abdication to the Government,
which was endorsed by the Parliament on the

00:08:29.830 --> 00:08:30.830
next day.

00:08:30.830 --> 00:08:34.399
He had become the only British monarch ever
to resign voluntarily.

00:08:34.399 --> 00:08:39.219
His brother Albert, Duke of York, would rise
to the throne as King George VI.

00:08:39.219 --> 00:08:42.800
On the 11th of December, the now former Edward
VIII announced his decision to the nation

00:08:42.800 --> 00:08:49.680
on the radio:
“A few hours ago I discharged my last duty

00:08:49.680 --> 00:09:03.750
as King and Emperor, and now that I have been
succeeded by my brother, the Duke of York,

00:09:03.750 --> 00:09:06.860
my first words must be to declare my allegiance
to him.

00:09:06.860 --> 00:09:08.850
This I do with all my heart.

00:09:08.850 --> 00:09:17.120
You all know the reasons which have impelled
me to renounce the throne.

00:09:17.120 --> 00:09:26.649
But I want you to understand that in making
up my mind I did not forget the country or

00:09:26.649 --> 00:09:36.519
the empire, which, as Prince of Wales and
lately as King, I have for twenty-five years

00:09:36.519 --> 00:09:38.589
tried to serve.

00:09:38.589 --> 00:09:49.550
But you must believe me when I tell you that
I have found it impossible to carry the heavy

00:09:49.550 --> 00:09:56.820
burden of responsibility and to discharge
my duties as King as I would wish to do without

00:09:56.820 --> 00:10:02.510
the help and support of the woman I love.

00:10:02.510 --> 00:10:11.360
And I want you to know that the decision I
have made has been mine and mine alone.

00:10:11.360 --> 00:10:18.100
This was a thing I had to judge entirely for
myself.

00:10:18.100 --> 00:10:30.500
The other person most nearly concerned has
tried up to the last to persuade me to take

00:10:30.500 --> 00:10:32.700
a different course.”

00:10:32.700 --> 00:10:36.200
This part was true, although at the time few
believed Edward’s words.

00:10:36.200 --> 00:10:40.190
Wallis was horrified at the idea that Edward
would abdicate for her, and tried to talk

00:10:40.190 --> 00:10:41.190
him out of it.

00:10:41.190 --> 00:10:43.160
Theirs had only been an affair, not real love.

00:10:43.160 --> 00:10:45.760
Wallis was still in love with Felipe and with
Herman.

00:10:45.760 --> 00:10:49.810
But once the abdication was announced, she
felt obliged to go through with the charade

00:10:49.810 --> 00:10:53.010
and marry Edward, now styled the Duke of Windsor.

00:10:53.010 --> 00:10:57.150
Biographer Andrew Morton reveals that on the
day before the wedding, she begged Herman,

00:10:57.150 --> 00:10:59.459
her one true love, to get her pregnant.

00:10:59.459 --> 00:11:04.010
This would not have been possible, as a previous
surgical operation had left Wallis incapable

00:11:04.010 --> 00:11:05.010
of conceiving.

00:11:05.010 --> 00:11:07.340
In any case, Herman refused her proposal.

00:11:07.340 --> 00:11:11.709
Edward and Wallis married on the 3rd of June
1937 at the Château de Cande, near Tours,

00:11:11.709 --> 00:11:12.709
in France.

00:11:12.709 --> 00:11:17.040
A Guardian article from the time reports that
Herman Rogers was present and very active

00:11:17.040 --> 00:11:18.040
at the ceremony.

00:11:18.040 --> 00:11:21.570
He played host to the groom, walked Wallis
to the altar and even delivered an address

00:11:21.570 --> 00:11:25.940
to the press on behalf of Edward, asking for
the couples’ privacy to be respected.

00:11:25.940 --> 00:11:30.040
Based on what we know today about Wallis and
Herman, the whole wedding must have felt incredibly

00:11:30.040 --> 00:11:32.440
awkward for the two souls.

00:11:32.440 --> 00:11:36.390
In any case, the marriage was sealed, and
the newlyweds left for a three month honeymoon

00:11:36.390 --> 00:11:37.550
in Austria.

00:11:37.550 --> 00:11:41.630
Yet again, based on Morton’s accounts, also
Wallis’ third marriage remained unconsummated.

00:11:41.630 --> 00:11:50.510
The Windsors and Nazi Germany
For the following two years, Wallis and Edward,

00:11:50.510 --> 00:11:52.769
or the Windsors, lived mainly in France.

00:11:52.769 --> 00:11:56.180
The relationships between them and the Royal
Family were severely strained.

00:11:56.180 --> 00:12:01.300
Edward was furious at George VI for refusing
to style Wallis as ‘Her Royal Highness’.

00:12:01.300 --> 00:12:05.230
On the other hand, George forbade his brother
to return to Britain, lest he rescind his

00:12:05.230 --> 00:12:06.459
generous allowance.

00:12:06.459 --> 00:12:10.279
The relationship with the Court, and with
the Cabinet, worsened when the Windsors visited

00:12:10.279 --> 00:12:11.900
Germany in October 1937.

00:12:11.900 --> 00:12:16.389
The official reason for the visit was for
Edward to learn more about the regime’s

00:12:16.389 --> 00:12:17.980
employment and housing policies.

00:12:17.980 --> 00:12:21.720
In truth, Edward was craving for a princely
treatment – especially for Wallis – and

00:12:21.720 --> 00:12:25.010
knew that the German leadership were all too
keen to roll out the red carpet.

00:12:25.010 --> 00:12:29.430
In addition to that, he saw himself as a mediator
between Britain and Germany

00:12:29.430 --> 00:12:33.940
During their visit the Windsors met with Goering,
Foreign Minister von Ribbentrop, Goebbels,

00:12:33.940 --> 00:12:35.339
Himmler, Rudolph Hess.

00:12:35.339 --> 00:12:39.139
They even spent two hours with the Fuhrer
and the Duke was photographed delivering a

00:12:39.139 --> 00:12:40.139
Nazi salute.

00:12:40.139 --> 00:12:43.360
The British Government saw this as an embarrassment.

00:12:43.360 --> 00:12:46.920
Two years later, after the declaration of
war on Germany, Edward was appointed liaison

00:12:46.920 --> 00:12:48.480
officer to the French Army.

00:12:48.480 --> 00:12:53.480
When the Germans finally invaded in May 1940,
the Windsors escaped to southern France, then

00:12:53.480 --> 00:12:57.280
on to neutral Spain and finally in Portugal
in July.

00:12:57.280 --> 00:13:00.530
New Prime Minister, Winston Churchill, was
keen to get the Windsors back to Britain as

00:13:00.530 --> 00:13:01.530
soon as possible.

00:13:01.530 --> 00:13:06.279
But the Duke delayed proceedings as he wanted
to negotiate a post befitting to his rank

00:13:06.279 --> 00:13:08.070
and “simple courtesies” for Wallis.

00:13:08.070 --> 00:13:09.070
In other words: recognition of their royal
status.

00:13:09.070 --> 00:13:11.610
Meanwhile, the Germans were conspiring to
get hold of the Windsors.

00:13:11.610 --> 00:13:16.910
In their plan, should the invasion of Britain
succeeded, Edward would have made a good collaborationist

00:13:16.910 --> 00:13:17.910
King.

00:13:17.910 --> 00:13:21.940
Their theory was confirmed by the intelligence
reports flying in from Madrid and Lisbon,

00:13:21.940 --> 00:13:24.790
according to which
“Windsor spoke strongly against Churchill

00:13:24.790 --> 00:13:27.130
and against this war,”
and that the Duke was

00:13:27.130 --> 00:13:32.310
“convinced that had he remained on the throne,
war would have been avoided and describes

00:13:32.310 --> 00:13:36.680
himself as a firm supporter of peaceful compromise
with Germany”

00:13:36.680 --> 00:13:39.449
The telegram from Lisbon continued with a
sinister note:

00:13:39.449 --> 00:13:44.190
“The Duke believes with certainty that continued
heavy bombing will make England ready for

00:13:44.190 --> 00:13:45.190
peace.”

00:13:45.190 --> 00:13:48.420
A certain show about the British Royal Family
builds on this idea, that Edward may have

00:13:48.420 --> 00:13:51.740
inspired the carpet bombing of civilian targets
in Britain.

00:13:51.740 --> 00:13:56.630
This is not verified and surely the Luftwaffe
did not need this passing comment to inspire

00:13:56.630 --> 00:13:58.170
their strategic plans.

00:13:58.170 --> 00:14:03.240
What we can say with more certainty though
is that Edward was potentially pro-Nazi, still

00:14:03.240 --> 00:14:09.320
believed in the 1930s politics of appeasement
and was a defeatist, to say the least.

00:14:09.320 --> 00:14:13.110
Churchill was aware of the danger of the Duke’s
declarations and so issued orders to keep

00:14:13.110 --> 00:14:16.890
an eye on him, lest he speak against the Allied
war effort to the press.

00:14:16.890 --> 00:14:20.449
He even sent him a direct warning, ordering
him not to mention

00:14:20.449 --> 00:14:25.000
“any view about the war, or about the Germans,
or about Hitlerism, which is different from

00:14:25.000 --> 00:14:28.019
that adopted by the British nation and Parliament.”

00:14:28.019 --> 00:14:30.279
But the Duke’s delaying tactics continued.

00:14:30.279 --> 00:14:33.850
Churchill’s ultimate solution was to get
the Duke as far away from Europe as possible

00:14:33.850 --> 00:14:36.389
by appointing him Governor of The Bahamas.

00:14:36.389 --> 00:14:41.870
But Edward refused, claiming the Bahamas to
be a “third-class British colony” and

00:14:41.870 --> 00:14:45.070
considered returning to Spain as Franco’s
guest instead.

00:14:45.070 --> 00:14:48.860
Von Ribbentrop took advantage of the stalling
situation and sent an explicit offer to the

00:14:48.860 --> 00:14:51.370
Windsors:
“Germany is determined to force England

00:14:51.370 --> 00:14:55.800
to peace by every means of power and upon
this happening would be prepared to accommodate

00:14:55.800 --> 00:15:00.410
any desire expressed by the Duke, especially
with a view to the assumption of the English

00:15:00.410 --> 00:15:02.600
throne by the Duke and Duchess.”

00:15:02.600 --> 00:15:07.060
The offer did not solicit an immediate agreement,
but neither a flat-out rejection.

00:15:07.060 --> 00:15:12.060
A German agent reported that the Duchess appeared
to be considering this proposal very thoughtfully.

00:15:12.060 --> 00:15:14.709
But this was not enough for Berlin.

00:15:14.709 --> 00:15:18.800
The Germans needed to take the Windsors back
to Madrid, to better control them, and so

00:15:18.800 --> 00:15:23.180
they launched a secret operation, codenamed
‘Willi’ to ensure that the Duke bent to

00:15:23.180 --> 00:15:24.210
their will.

00:15:24.210 --> 00:15:28.480
The head of Nazi counterintelligence, Walter
Schellenberg travelled to Lisbon to oversee

00:15:28.480 --> 00:15:29.480
the simple plan.

00:15:29.480 --> 00:15:34.120
It involved a threatening message relayed
by Miguel Primo de Rivera, a Spanish nationalist

00:15:34.120 --> 00:15:35.329
very close to Franco.

00:15:35.329 --> 00:15:39.750
De Rivera informed the Windsors that they
had uncovered a British plot to murder them

00:15:39.750 --> 00:15:42.310
and urged them to seek Spain’s protection.

00:15:42.310 --> 00:15:44.089
Eventually, Plan Willi failed.

00:15:44.089 --> 00:15:47.410
Winston Churchill threatened the Duke with
a Court Martial if he refused to accept the

00:15:47.410 --> 00:15:48.509
post in the Bahamas.

00:15:48.509 --> 00:15:53.279
Edward was after all a Major-General of the
Army, and subject to military justice.

00:15:53.279 --> 00:15:56.490
The Windsors eventually left for the Bahamas
on August 1, 1940.

00:15:56.490 --> 00:16:01.480
The Duke may have still been wary of the warnings
from De Rivera, as he demanded a Scotland

00:16:01.480 --> 00:16:02.480
Yard detective for personal protection.

00:16:02.480 --> 00:16:06.180
Edward had been physically removed from the
war in Europe, but he still managed to score

00:16:06.180 --> 00:16:08.089
more embarrassing situations.

00:16:08.089 --> 00:16:11.769
In late 1940, for example, when interviewed
by an American magazine, he said:

00:16:11.769 --> 00:16:15.600
“There will be no revolution in Germany
and it would be a tragic thing for the world

00:16:15.600 --> 00:16:17.509
if Hitler were to be overthrown.

00:16:17.509 --> 00:16:21.670
Hitler is the right and logical leader of
the German people… Hitler is a very great

00:16:21.670 --> 00:16:22.670
man.”

00:16:22.670 --> 00:16:26.420
As a Governor of the Bahamas, Edward disliked
the job, but he did some good work there.

00:16:26.420 --> 00:16:30.949
The Duke launched initiatives to raise wages
for Bahamians and promoted plans to relieve

00:16:30.949 --> 00:16:31.949
poverty.

00:16:31.949 --> 00:16:35.010
But true to character, he could not help yet
another faux pas.

00:16:35.010 --> 00:16:37.120
As he addressed these social problems, he
blamed them on

00:16:37.120 --> 00:16:42.319
“men of Central European Jewish descent,
who had secured jobs as a pretext for obtaining

00:16:42.319 --> 00:16:44.570
a deferment of draft.”

00:16:44.570 --> 00:16:52.370
Life after the War[TA4]
Full reports on the whole relationship between

00:16:52.370 --> 00:16:56.870
the Windsors and Nazi Germany were collected
after the War in the so-called Windsor File.

00:16:56.870 --> 00:17:00.810
Most of it, however, remains classified, and
for the moment we will not know the full extent

00:17:00.810 --> 00:17:04.390
of the Duke’s sympathy or even collaboration
with the regime.

00:17:04.390 --> 00:17:07.180
What we have, are his public declarations
and interviews.

00:17:07.180 --> 00:17:12.570
In the 1950s he blamed WWII on
“anti-appeasement politicians in Britain,

00:17:12.570 --> 00:17:17.000
Roosevelt and the Jews”
In 1966 he told an American newspaper the

00:17:17.000 --> 00:17:19.360
believed the UK should not have intervened.

00:17:19.360 --> 00:17:24.450
“I thought the rest of us could be fence
sitters while the Nazis and the Reds slogged

00:17:24.450 --> 00:17:26.840
it out,”
And privately, he expressed the view that

00:17:26.840 --> 00:17:32.190
“I never thought Hitler was such a bad chap,”
After the war the Windsors lived between the

00:17:32.190 --> 00:17:33.190
US and France.

00:17:33.190 --> 00:17:37.160
On the outside, it appeared as if Wallis and
the former king lived a happy life of leisure,

00:17:37.160 --> 00:17:39.310
thanks to the allowance still granted to them.

00:17:39.310 --> 00:17:44.550
But the reality was not as it seemed, as Andrew
Morton has uncovered by reviewing Wallis’

00:17:44.550 --> 00:17:45.550
letters.

00:17:45.550 --> 00:17:48.660
Edward was always besotted, completely infatuated
with her.

00:17:48.660 --> 00:17:52.980
Wallis always treated him with deference and
respect, at least in public.

00:17:52.980 --> 00:17:56.350
She always referred to him as ‘the Duke’
and called him ‘Sir’ in public.

00:17:56.350 --> 00:17:58.400
But in private, things were very different.

00:17:58.400 --> 00:18:02.800
She spent her days being unpleasant to all
those around her, and treated with harsh coldness

00:18:02.800 --> 00:18:06.540
especially her husband, to whom she denied
any intimate contact.

00:18:06.540 --> 00:18:10.270
He would often say to her, ‘Am I going to
go to bed in tears again tonight?’

00:18:10.270 --> 00:18:13.260
According to Morton, all they shared in later
life was

00:18:13.260 --> 00:18:18.310
“a mutual interest in golf, fascism and
casual racism”

00:18:18.310 --> 00:18:22.520
Wallis never forgot her first true loves,
for Felipe, but especially for Herman.

00:18:22.520 --> 00:18:26.520
Many of the letters uncovered by Morton, in
fact are addressed to him.

00:18:26.520 --> 00:18:31.800
When Herman and Felipe died, Wallis was devastated,
and she retreated from the world paying little

00:18:31.800 --> 00:18:33.270
to no attention to her husband.

00:18:33.270 --> 00:18:37.880
Edward paid only short visits to Britain after
the War, mainly to attend funerals of family

00:18:37.880 --> 00:18:38.880
members.

00:18:38.880 --> 00:18:41.470
His relationship with the Royal Family remained
strained and distant.

00:18:41.470 --> 00:18:46.250
His health deteriorated and in the early 1970s
Edward was diagnosed with throat cancer.

00:18:46.250 --> 00:18:51.170
During his dying moments, on the 28th of May
1972, the former King was being comforted

00:18:51.170 --> 00:18:52.200
by a nurse.

00:18:52.200 --> 00:18:56.370
With his last breath he whispered
‘Wallis, Wallis and Wallis,’

00:18:56.370 --> 00:18:57.780
But Wallis was not there.

00:18:57.780 --> 00:19:03.350
Edward died in the arms of a stranger, rather
than the woman for whom he had abdicated.

00:19:03.350 --> 00:19:05.740
After the Duke’s death, Wallis settled in
Paris.

00:19:05.740 --> 00:19:09.740
Once a known socialite, described as one of
the best dressed women in the world, Wallis

00:19:09.740 --> 00:19:12.490
retreated from the world and stopped entertaining.

00:19:12.490 --> 00:19:16.890
She died of coronary heart disease on the
24th of April 1986.

00:19:16.890 --> 00:19:23.080
Without any heirs nor relatives, she left
her estate to the Pasteur research institute.

00:19:23.080 --> 00:19:29.940
What if … ?
And so ends one of the saddest personal stories

00:19:29.940 --> 00:19:31.330
of the XXth Century.

00:19:31.330 --> 00:19:35.730
As Wallis and Edward’s lives have been told
on film many times I will not ask you to suggest

00:19:35.730 --> 00:19:38.760
a cast for yet another biopic.

00:19:38.760 --> 00:19:43.290
But rather to consider this: considering what
we know about Edward’s pro-appeasement and

00:19:43.290 --> 00:19:47.480
potentially pro-Nazi stance, what would have
happened if he had not abdicated?

00:19:47.480 --> 00:19:50.860
Could an extended reign of Edward VIII have
changed the course of history?

00:19:50.860 --> 00:19:53.760
How would have WWII played out in such a scenario?

00:19:53.760 --> 00:19:58.430
Please post your alternative histories in
the comments …. And as usual, thank you

00:19:58.430 --> 00:19:59.140
for watching.

